Z||000000||GREENDANO||07-03-99||07:17 AM||||Does any one have the part number for the GM front upper control arms that was suppossed to fix/help bump steer?<br>  Thanks!<br>     Dan-O
Z||000001||batboy||07-03-99||07:43 AM||||Sorry Dan-o, I don't know the answer to your question. Just wanted to make a comment about this bump steering thing though. I find all this talk about it kind of humorous. You want to know REAL bump steering? Go drive an old 1950 Chevy truck with the orginal front straight axle and worn out king pins. LOL, now that's truly bump steering and why I swapped a Camaro front subframe into my old pickup. The Fieros handle like a dream compared to some of the antique cars and trucks that I've driven in the past. Those old straight axle vehicles wandered all over the road and caught every seam and crack in the pavement. Ok, I'll shut up now.
Z||000002||lowCG||07-03-99||10:14 AM||||I wrote'em down(R-14094450,L-14094449),but really wonder what they can offer besides more range of adjustment(they offer up to/additional 1.6 DEG of positive camber).<br>Pretty hard to change the geometry by replacing something that only connects two pivot points.
Z||000003||GREENDANO||07-04-99||07:12 AM||||I agree with you,LOW, I didn't see how one control arm swap without realy changing the geometry was going to help bumpsteer.  But I figured I'd snag the parts before the were discontinued and toss 'em on over the winter.  I'm contimplating lowering the whole car, and would like to still have the same "normal" ride quality I have now.<br>  Batboy, I too feel your pain.  My 83 Pro-street Ranger with a big block has manual steering with lowered I-beams.  Big Mickey Thompsons on the back and skinny ones on the front.  HAIRY RIDE!!!  Let's just say, next car I build will have a similar set-up to what you have under the front of your truck.<br>  BTW, to everybody else, I saw a picture of Batboy's truck.  This is the defininition of street-rod.  It has all the right combos, plus he actually drives it!!  <br>  Dan-O
